// games/sonic4/test/poison/poison_band_buried_tint.emp — POISON (see README).
//
// TARGETS: OWN-1's bury rule — an op with no band id writing an entry a band is live on
//          (band-ownership design §3.2; the successor to guard C-A's "would bury").
// EXPECTED FRAGMENT: "would bury"
//
// A band whose OFF edge would bury an unrelated tint. The band runs 100..140 over CRAM
// $48..$4D; at line 120, INSIDE it, an unbanded fire writes $4A..$4D. From line 140 down
// those two colours are replaced by the frame's base palette, so an effect the author
// scheduled for the bottom of the screen silently stops at 140 — and nothing at runtime
// can report that, because the restore is doing exactly what a restore does.
//
// WHAT CHANGED AT PARCEL P1, and it is worth reading before editing this file. The old
// spelling was three hand-authored fires — an equal-span "partner" at 100, this
// intersecting tint at 120, and a bare `pal_restore` at 140 — and the guard had to work
// out which of the first two was the partner by comparing spans. The unequal span was
// LOAD-BEARING then: an intersecting op with the SAME span was a partner candidate and
// tripped a different ensure. It is no longer load-bearing at all. The band declares its
// own two ops now, so ANY op without that band's id writing an entry the band is live on
// is buried, equal span or not. The unequal span is kept because it is the shape the
// original defect had, not because the guard needs it.
//
// COUNT 2, and the derivation is the walk's: the intersecting write covers CRAM entries
// 37 and 38, the walk carries one `poisoned` flag PER ENTRY, and so it says the same true
// thing once for each entry it is true of. (The old count of 2 was two DIFFERENT guards —
// the bury ensure and an intersecting-op tally — firing once each. Same number, different
// reason.)
//
// NO IMPORTS — see poison_four_bands.emp's note.
module games.sonic4.test.poison.poison_band_buried_tint

const POISON_BAND_BURIED_TINT = compose([
    band(100, 140, stream_cram($48, [$0EEE, $0AAA, $0666]), 0),
    [ fire(120, [stream_cram($4A, [$0E00, $0A00])]) ],
])

ensure(raster_program(POISON_BAND_BURIED_TINT).len > 0,
       "poison_band_buried_tint: raster_program returned nothing — this poison no longer reaches the guard it targets")
